<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a heap-buffer-overflow in the `skip_redundant_chars` function in `parser_yang.c`. The `while` loop at line 4732 checks for a null terminator to stop processing, but it does not check against the provided length of the input buffer. When the fuzzer provides a non-null-terminated string, the function reads past the end of the allocated buffer, leading to a crash.

The fix is to modify the loop condition in `skip_redundant_chars` to also check the remaining length of the input buffer. The `ly_in_left()` function can be used for this purpose.

The corrected code would look something like this:

```c
static LY_ERR
skip_redundant_chars(struct lysp_yang_ctx *ctx)
{
/* read some trailing spaces, new lines, or comments */
while (ly_in_left(ctx->in) > 0) {
if (!strncmp(ctx->in->current, "//", 2)) {
/* one-line comment */
ly_in_skip(ctx->in, 2);
LY_CHECK_RET(skip_comment(ctx, 1));
} else if (!strncmp(ctx->in->current, "/*", 2)) {
/* block comment */
ly_in_skip(ctx->in, 2);
LY_CHECK_RET(skip_comment(ctx, 2));
} else if (isspace(ctx->in->current[0])) {
/* whitespace */
ly_in_skip(ctx->in, 1);
} else {
/* other character */
break;
}
}

return LY_SUCCESS;
}
```
This change ensures that the function will not read beyond the provided input data, thus preventing the buffer overflow.
</crash-analysis>

